1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,600
In early September 2014, in Monrovia, the capital of

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,062
Liberia, taxis filled with entire families circled

3
00:00:08,062 --> 00:00:10,832
the city, searching for a treatment bed.

4
00:00:11,282 --> 00:00:14,490
Liberia was reporting 300 to 400 new Ebola cases

5
00:00:14,490 --> 00:00:15,225
every week.

6
00:00:15,225 --> 00:00:18,500
At one point, the World Health Organization says,

7
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:21,708
there were virtually no treatment beds available

8
00:00:21,708 --> 00:00:23,312
anywhere in the country.

9
00:00:23,762 --> 00:00:27,650
That September, a CDC model warned that, without

10
00:00:27,650 --> 00:00:31,863
more action, Liberia and Sierra Leone could reach an

11
00:00:31,863 --> 00:00:35,022
estimated 1.4 million cases by January.

12
00:00:35,472 --> 00:00:39,325
8 months later, on May 9, 2015, the World Health

13
00:00:39,325 --> 00:00:43,899
Organization declared Liberia free of Ebola transmission.

14
00:00:44,299 --> 00:00:47,636
So how was the largest Ebola epidemic in history

15
00:00:47,636 --> 00:00:48,957
stopped in Liberia?

16
00:00:48,957 --> 00:00:52,086
The answer is not the one most people expect.

17
00:00:52,486 --> 00:00:54,255
Stopping Ebola.

18
00:00:54,255 --> 00:00:55,905
Liberia, 2014.

19
00:00:58,305 --> 00:01:00,936
To understand how it was stopped, start with how it

20
00:01:00,936 --> 00:01:01,349
spreads.

21
00:01:02,349 --> 00:01:06,146
Ebola viruses were discovered in 1976, in what is

22
00:01:06,146 --> 00:01:09,323
now the Democratic Republic of the Congo.

23
00:01:09,323 --> 00:01:12,810
The epidemic in West Africa was caused by one

24
00:01:12,810 --> 00:01:14,825
species, Zaire ebolavirus.

25
00:01:15,275 --> 00:01:18,108
According to the CDC, people get Ebola through

26
00:01:18,108 --> 00:01:21,310
contact with the body fluids of a person who is sick

27
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:23,343
with it, or who has died from it.

28
00:01:23,343 --> 00:01:26,607
It does not spread through the air, the way flu does.

29
00:01:27,057 --> 00:01:30,457
Symptoms can appear 2 to 21 days after contact.

30
00:01:30,457 --> 00:01:32,266
On average, 8 to 10 days.

31
00:01:32,666 --> 00:01:35,579
That put two kinds of work at the front line.

32
00:01:35,579 --> 00:01:36,875
Caring for the sick.

33
00:01:36,875 --> 00:01:38,234
And burying the dead.

34
00:01:38,734 --> 00:01:42,686
According to the CDC, the first case was reported in

35
00:01:42,686 --> 00:01:44,585
Guinea, in December 2013.

36
00:01:44,585 --> 00:01:48,537
On March 30, 2014, Ebola was reported in Liberia, in

37
00:01:48,537 --> 00:01:49,449
Lofa County.

38
00:01:49,899 --> 00:01:52,641
Then it reached the capital. Monrovia.

39
00:01:53,241 --> 00:01:55,747
And the capital had almost nowhere to put the sick.

40
00:01:56,747 --> 00:02:00,328
By mid-July 2014, Liberia had only two Ebola

41
00:02:00,328 --> 00:02:03,421
treatment units running. 20 beds each.

42
00:02:03,871 --> 00:02:07,403
In mid-August, a WHO team estimated that 1,000 beds

43
00:02:07,403 --> 00:02:11,004
were needed, just for the patients already infected.

44
00:02:11,004 --> 00:02:12,666
Only 240 were available.

45
00:02:13,116 --> 00:02:16,977
On August 17, Doctors Without Borders opened a new

46
00:02:16,977 --> 00:02:20,684
120-bed Ebola center in Monrovia, called ELWA 3.

47
00:02:20,684 --> 00:02:23,695
In its first week, it was already full.

48
00:02:24,145 --> 00:02:25,884
Patients were turned away.

49
00:02:25,884 --> 00:02:29,228
Many died on hospital grounds, in city streets, or

50
00:02:29,228 --> 00:02:30,231
in their homes.

51
00:02:30,831 --> 00:02:33,588
Health workers were hit hard.

52
00:02:33,588 --> 00:02:37,771
In Liberia, 378 were infected, and 192 died.

53
00:02:37,771 --> 00:02:43,095
The country lost 8% of its doctors, nurses and midwives.

54
00:02:43,695 --> 00:02:45,778
Then, the United States made a decision.

55
00:02:46,778 --> 00:02:50,268
On September 16, 2014, President Barack Obama spoke

56
00:02:50,268 --> 00:02:51,774
at the CDC in Atlanta.

57
00:02:51,774 --> 00:02:54,100
He described the epidemic bluntly.

58
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:56,769
It's spiraling out of control, he said.

59
00:02:56,769 --> 00:02:58,138
It is getting worse.

60
00:02:58,588 --> 00:03:01,722
He announced a military command center in Liberia,

61
00:03:01,722 --> 00:03:04,856
to support civilian efforts across the region, and

62
00:03:04,856 --> 00:03:07,865
an air bridge to get health workers and supplies

63
00:03:07,865 --> 00:03:09,369
into West Africa faster.

64
00:03:09,369 --> 00:03:12,127
The White House said an estimated 3,000 U.S.

65
00:03:12,127 --> 00:03:13,130
forces would go.

66
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:17,202
But the Pentagon was clear about one thing. The U.S.

67
00:03:17,202 --> 00:03:21,171
military would not provide direct care to Ebola patients.

68
00:03:21,571 --> 00:03:24,804
Its job was the kind of work militaries do well.

69
00:03:24,804 --> 00:03:27,701
Moving things. Testing. Building. Training.

70
00:03:28,151 --> 00:03:31,365
The mission was called Operation United Assistance.

71
00:03:31,365 --> 00:03:34,328
It was the Defense Department's part of a wider

72
00:03:34,328 --> 00:03:35,777
effort, led by the U.S.

73
00:03:35,777 --> 00:03:38,110
Agency for International Development.

74
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:39,913
Engineers went too.

75
00:03:39,913 --> 00:03:43,616
At Fort Hood, Texas, the 36th Engineer Brigade cased

76
00:03:43,616 --> 00:03:46,821
its colors and prepared to deploy to Liberia.

77
00:03:47,271 --> 00:03:50,511
In late October, the headquarters of the 101st

78
00:03:50,511 --> 00:03:54,102
Airborne Division took over the command in Liberia,

79
00:03:54,102 --> 00:03:56,426
under Major General Gary Volesky.

80
00:03:56,426 --> 00:03:59,454
At the peak, about 2,800 Defense Department

81
00:03:59,454 --> 00:04:02,201
personnel were deployed to West Africa.

82
00:04:02,801 --> 00:04:03,832
And they went to work.

83
00:04:04,832 --> 00:04:05,799
First, testing.

84
00:04:05,799 --> 00:04:08,957
Early in the epidemic, confirming a case of Ebola

85
00:04:08,957 --> 00:04:10,826
could take as long as a week.

86
00:04:11,276 --> 00:04:14,669
On September 29, mobile labs from the Navy's Naval

87
00:04:14,669 --> 00:04:17,519
Medical Research Center landed in Liberia.

88
00:04:17,519 --> 00:04:21,183
By October 2, they were receiving samples in Monrovia.

89
00:04:21,633 --> 00:04:25,099
They cut the wait for a result from 7 days to less

90
00:04:25,099 --> 00:04:26,000
than 4 hours.

91
00:04:26,450 --> 00:04:27,908
Army labs joined them.

92
00:04:27,908 --> 00:04:31,354
The command set up 4 Army mobile testing labs across

93
00:04:31,354 --> 00:04:31,884
Liberia.

94
00:04:31,884 --> 00:04:35,131
Here, soldiers of the 1st Area Medical Laboratory

95
00:04:35,131 --> 00:04:37,650
pack up their lab before heading home.

96
00:04:38,050 --> 00:04:39,593
Second, building. U.S.

97
00:04:39,593 --> 00:04:43,031
Army engineers built Ebola treatment units, often

98
00:04:43,031 --> 00:04:46,259
working alongside the Armed Forces of Liberia.

99
00:04:46,709 --> 00:04:50,136
Near Sinje, Liberian army engineers worked with

100
00:04:50,136 --> 00:04:53,052
soldiers of the 62nd Engineer Battalion.

101
00:04:53,502 --> 00:04:57,098
At Gbediah, in December, the 104th Engineer Company

102
00:04:57,098 --> 00:05:00,200
built a treatment unit with Liberian troops.

103
00:05:00,650 --> 00:05:03,649
Some sites were so far out that Army helicopter

104
00:05:03,649 --> 00:05:06,648
crews used a refueling method called Fat Cow, a

105
00:05:06,648 --> 00:05:09,838
helicopter carrying fuel to the field, just to fly

106
00:05:09,838 --> 00:05:11,433
far enough to reach them.

107
00:05:11,883 --> 00:05:14,766
Chinooks flew supplies to treatment units like the

108
00:05:14,766 --> 00:05:17,187
one in Ganta, near the border with Guinea.

109
00:05:17,637 --> 00:05:20,890
On November 11, the treatment unit at Tubmanburg

110
00:05:20,890 --> 00:05:22,110
officially opened.

111
00:05:22,110 --> 00:05:25,430
It was built by the Armed Forces of Liberia, with

112
00:05:25,430 --> 00:05:27,802
support and oversight from the U.S.

113
00:05:27,802 --> 00:05:29,292
Department of Defense.

114
00:05:29,692 --> 00:05:31,880
Third, a hospital for the healers.

115
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:35,226
Navy Seabees, soldiers and airmen built the Monrovia

116
00:05:35,226 --> 00:05:36,063
Medical Unit.

117
00:05:36,063 --> 00:05:38,958
25 beds, for health workers who caught Ebola.

118
00:05:39,408 --> 00:05:42,498
It was run not by the military, but by the U.S.

119
00:05:42,498 --> 00:05:45,523
Public Health Service Commissioned Corps, in 4

120
00:05:45,523 --> 00:05:47,035
rotations of 70 people.

121
00:05:47,035 --> 00:05:50,060
It was the only Ebola treatment unit in Africa

122
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:52,230
dedicated to health care workers.

123
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:54,133
Fourth, training. U.S.

124
00:05:54,133 --> 00:05:57,303
trainers taught health workers how to put on and

125
00:05:57,303 --> 00:06:00,208
take off protective equipment, step by step.

126
00:06:00,658 --> 00:06:03,145
And they walked them through mock treatment wards,

127
00:06:03,145 --> 00:06:04,239
before the real thing.

128
00:06:04,689 --> 00:06:07,678
By the end of the mission, the division said it had

129
00:06:07,678 --> 00:06:10,902
trained more than 1,500 health care workers in Liberia.

130
00:06:11,352 --> 00:06:14,523
In all, the 101st said its joint force built and

131
00:06:14,523 --> 00:06:17,166
supported 17 treatment units in Liberia.

132
00:06:17,166 --> 00:06:20,404
Other official counts are lower, depending on who

133
00:06:20,404 --> 00:06:21,527
built which unit.

134
00:06:22,127 --> 00:06:24,765
And here is the part of the story that often gets

135
00:06:24,765 --> 00:06:25,249
left out.

136
00:06:26,249 --> 00:06:29,884
Liberia's epidemic had started to slow before most

137
00:06:29,884 --> 00:06:31,483
of those units opened.

138
00:06:31,483 --> 00:06:34,900
A CDC report published in November 2014 noted a

139
00:06:34,900 --> 00:06:37,662
decrease in cases since mid-September.

140
00:06:38,112 --> 00:06:41,513
By late September, treatment-bed capacity in Liberia

141
00:06:41,513 --> 00:06:43,999
had overtaken the number of new cases.

142
00:06:43,999 --> 00:06:47,400
And as the epidemic waned, many treatment units were

143
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:49,624
underused, and three never opened.

144
00:06:50,074 --> 00:06:54,058
In 2015, The New York Times reported that the 11

145
00:06:54,058 --> 00:06:56,050
treatment units the U.S.

146
00:06:56,050 --> 00:07:00,367
built in Liberia had treated only 28 Ebola patients.

147
00:07:00,967 --> 00:07:03,009
So what actually turned the tide?

148
00:07:03,009 --> 00:07:06,104
Researchers and health agencies point to Liberians

149
00:07:06,104 --> 00:07:06,785
themselves.

150
00:07:07,235 --> 00:07:10,911
A study in the CDC's journal, Emerging Infectious

151
00:07:10,911 --> 00:07:12,787
Diseases, puts it simply.

152
00:07:12,787 --> 00:07:16,689
Community engagement resulted in remarkable behavior

153
00:07:16,689 --> 00:07:17,214
change.

154
00:07:17,664 --> 00:07:18,750
Burials changed.

155
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:22,211
Because a body can still spread Ebola, safe burials

156
00:07:22,211 --> 00:07:22,821
mattered.

157
00:07:22,821 --> 00:07:26,011
Liberia went from fewer than 10 burial teams in

158
00:07:26,011 --> 00:07:29,676
August, to 54 trained and equipped teams by October 5.

159
00:07:30,126 --> 00:07:32,875
Teams tried to trace everyone who had been in

160
00:07:32,875 --> 00:07:34,280
contact with a patient.

161
00:07:34,280 --> 00:07:37,396
The same study notes that contact tracing generally

162
00:07:37,396 --> 00:07:39,901
depended on paper and Excel spreadsheets.

163
00:07:40,351 --> 00:07:44,112
And isolation worked, even outside the big treatment

164
00:07:44,112 --> 00:07:44,546
units.

165
00:07:44,546 --> 00:07:48,306
The CDC estimated that, from September 23 to October

166
00:07:48,306 --> 00:07:52,067
31, isolating patients in treatment units, community

167
00:07:52,067 --> 00:07:55,683
care centers, and similar settings prevented about

168
00:07:55,683 --> 00:07:57,346
9,000 cases in Liberia.

169
00:07:57,796 --> 00:08:00,532
So what did the U.S. military add?

170
00:08:00,532 --> 00:08:05,119
The Emerging Infectious Diseases study gives its verdict.

171
00:08:05,119 --> 00:08:07,211
The deployment of the U.S.

172
00:08:07,211 --> 00:08:11,475
military provided a logistic and psychological boost.

173
00:08:12,075 --> 00:08:13,605
Week by week, the numbers fell.

174
00:08:14,605 --> 00:08:18,433
By March 2015, Liberia was down to fewer than 2 new

175
00:08:18,433 --> 00:08:19,409
cases a week.

176
00:08:19,859 --> 00:08:24,011
The last confirmed patient was buried on March 28.

177
00:08:24,011 --> 00:08:28,247
42 days later, twice the longest incubation period,

178
00:08:28,247 --> 00:08:32,233
on May 9, 2015, the WHO declared Liberia free of

179
00:08:32,233 --> 00:08:33,811
Ebola transmission.

180
00:08:34,261 --> 00:08:35,686
It wasn't quite over.

181
00:08:35,686 --> 00:08:38,536
The virus flared up again, more than once.

182
00:08:38,536 --> 00:08:41,997
The WHO declared the end of Liberia's last outbreak

183
00:08:41,997 --> 00:08:43,083
on June 9, 2016.

184
00:08:43,483 --> 00:08:44,538
By then, the U.S.

185
00:08:44,538 --> 00:08:46,152
troops had long gone home.

186
00:08:46,152 --> 00:08:49,132
Before boarding, they went through final medical

187
00:08:49,132 --> 00:08:49,752
screening.

188
00:08:49,752 --> 00:08:52,732
Back in the United States, each spent 21 days in

189
00:08:52,732 --> 00:08:54,098
controlled monitoring.

190
00:08:54,548 --> 00:08:59,283
In February 2015, the 101st cased its colors in Monrovia.

191
00:08:59,283 --> 00:09:01,028
General Volesky said:

192
00:09:01,028 --> 00:09:05,265
the fight to get to zero Ebola cases will continue.

193
00:09:05,765 --> 00:09:10,728
Across Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone, the

194
00:09:10,728 --> 00:09:16,255
epidemic caused about 28,600 cases, and more than

195
00:09:16,255 --> 00:09:17,834
11,300 deaths.

196
00:09:17,834 --> 00:09:23,022
In Liberia alone, according to the CDC, 10,678

197
00:09:23,022 --> 00:09:25,729
cases, and 4,810 deaths.

198
00:09:26,529 --> 00:09:28,236
What changed after Liberia?

199
00:09:29,236 --> 00:09:32,411
In 2015, a vaccine trial in Guinea tested ring

200
00:09:32,411 --> 00:09:36,070
vaccination, vaccinating people around each new case.

201
00:09:36,070 --> 00:09:39,108
Its first results suggested high protection.

202
00:09:39,558 --> 00:09:43,669
In December 2019, the FDA approved Ervebo, the first

203
00:09:43,669 --> 00:09:46,119
FDA-approved vaccine for Ebola.

204
00:09:46,119 --> 00:09:50,546
In 2020, it approved two treatments, Inmazeb and Ebanga.

205
00:09:50,996 --> 00:09:53,866
But all three are for one species, Zaire ebolavirus.

206
00:09:53,866 --> 00:09:56,073
And today, a different one is spreading.

207
00:09:56,473 --> 00:10:00,765
In May 2026, the Democratic Republic of the Congo

208
00:10:00,765 --> 00:10:05,846
and Uganda declared outbreaks of Bundibugyo virus disease.

209
00:10:05,846 --> 00:10:10,138
As of November 6, the CDC counts 10,910 confirmed

210
00:10:10,138 --> 00:10:14,780
cases, and 5,306 deaths, almost all of them in Congo.

211
00:10:15,230 --> 00:10:18,664
In June, the CDC reported that no medications or

212
00:10:18,664 --> 00:10:21,382
vaccines against it had been approved.

213
00:10:21,382 --> 00:10:24,887
The CDC also says the risk to the American public

214
00:10:24,887 --> 00:10:25,745
remains low.

215
00:10:26,245 --> 00:10:31,119
The tools that worked in Liberia still matter. Fast labs.

216
00:10:31,119 --> 00:10:32,744
Beds. Safe burials.

217
00:10:32,744 --> 00:10:34,112
Contact tracing.

218
00:10:34,112 --> 00:10:36,592
And the trust of communities.

219
00:10:37,042 --> 00:10:40,516
In 2014, the help that came from outside was real.

220
00:10:40,516 --> 00:10:44,267
But when Liberia reached zero, the WHO put it plainly.

221
00:10:44,267 --> 00:10:48,157
It is a tribute to the government and people of Liberia.

222
00:10:49,057 --> 00:10:51,422
If this story surprised you, subscribe.

223
00:10:51,422 --> 00:10:53,606
Next time on KEPT ALIVE: penicillin.

224
00:10:53,606 --> 00:10:55,183
The mold that went to war.

225
00:10:55,983 --> 00:10:57,123
For education only.

226
00:10:57,123 --> 00:10:58,744
This is not medical advice.

227
00:10:58,744 --> 00:11:01,566
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
